  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "The full paper is present. Some supplementary figure text, especially Figure 8, is garbled, but the relevant Table 2 values and accompanying prose are readable.",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study reports residual concept reproduction and prompt-channel bypass after an opaque post-condition in diffusion models, extending the prompt-based recovery question to a different model family. It does not test LLM unlearning methods.",
    "gate_note": "The study tests an archived, opaque, indivisible post-condition rather than a named unlearning method. It reports fixed-CLIP operational event frequencies for residual reproduction and prompt bypass, including obfuscated prompts. It does not test fine-tuning, activation ablation, quantization, or general LLM unlearning. Chance-level performance and general capability retention are not evaluated. Other cited work is not counted as study results.",
    "test_reason": "No version is tested. The study audits text-to-image diffusion models (SD1.5/2.1/SDXL), not LLMs. Its intervention is one opaque, indivisible archived post-condition. The authors say it supports no checkpoint-, filter- or optimizer-level attribution, so it cannot be identified as an unlearning method. The versions concern unlearned knowledge in LLMs, so the rows measure a related construct in a different kind of system. Unlearning and erasure brittleness appear only in related work, through cited studies.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors present CLRC as an audit and reporting schema, not an erasure method. Risk varies by concept family, channel, backbone and protocol, and standard-prompt evaluation understates risk. They argue residual reproduction, bypass and benign spillover should be reported jointly. Post-condition results describe an opaque archived condition with no attribution to a method.",
    "recipe": "none",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper proposes an audit schema and evaluates an opaque archived post-condition; it does not reproduce or implement a named LLM unlearning recipe.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Reported risk varies by conditioning channel and attack protocol; obfuscated prompt bypass exceeds standard prompt bypass in the reported aggregate.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "In SD1.5, after an opaque archived post-condition, fixed-CLIP-judge reproduction of target concepts falls by about half, from 0.32 to 0.14 (prompt) and from 0.58 to 0.29 (embedding). Obfuscated prompts raise reproduction to 0.19, above the 0.14 residual. Standard attack prompts give 0.11, below the residual. Benign loss D is non-zero (0.031 standard, 0.049 obfuscated).",
        "proposed_claim": "Concept suppression in text-to-image diffusion models is incomplete, and obfuscated prompts partly restore suppressed concepts.",
